  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry weather pattern continues, with \nsmall chances for rain Thursday night and late this weekend.\nNear record warmth Thursday.\n\nA surface ridge will continue to affect our region this week as\nembedded high pressure center moves from northern ON toward \nnorthern New England,while the ridge aloft builds from the \nUpper Midwest to the eastern Great Lakes. Dry weather is \nexpected as stabilizing subsidence accompanies the ridge.\n\nDecent CAA and a sharp decrease in low level thicknesses will\nlead to near or just below normal conditions tomorrow, with\nhighs mainly in the 50s. Thereafter, heights build Wednesday and\nThursday with temperatures approaching near record levels. Highs\non Wednesday will reach the mid to upper 60, and upper 60s to \nlower 70s Thursday ahead of the next frontal system.\n\nA weak cold front should sweep eastward through our region \nThursday night. Periodic rain showers are possible along and \nahead of this front Thursday night due to moist isentropic \nascent ahead of a shortwave trough axis aloft and low-level \nconvergence/ascent along the front coinciding with sufficient \nlow-level moisture. Well above normal conditions will continue \nbehind this system into the weekend, with dry conditions \nreturning until late Sunday.",
